// batch v: yorktown, valmy, piramides, copenhague, assaye, trafalgar
Object.assign(GLOSSARY, {
  "navio de linha": "The great wooden warship of the age of sail, with two or three gun decks (from 64 to over 100 guns). Two fleets of ships of the line faced each other in line, side by side.",
  "linha de batalha": "A formation in which ships follow one another in single file, so that all of them fire at once with the guns of one side. It was the standard naval tactic for more than 150 years.",
  "sotavento": "The side toward which the wind blows, the opposite of windward. A ship to leeward is 'below' the wind relative to the other.",
  "fogo em enfiada": "Fire delivered along the length of a ship, from the stern or the bow. The shot travels through the whole ship, and the one firing gets no reply, because the enemy's guns point to the sides.",
  "carronada": "A short, heavy cannon, of short range and terrible effect up close. It fired large balls or grapeshot against the crew on deck.",
  "fragata": "A sailing warship, smaller and faster than the ship of the line. It served to scout, carry messages and accompany the fleet.",
  "bateria flutuante": "An anchored hull loaded with cannons, used to defend a port. It does not move, but it is a firing platform that is hard to sink.",
  "baioneta": "A blade fixed to the tip of a musket or rifle, which turns the weapon into a spear for hand-to-hand fighting.",
  "vau": "A shallow stretch of a river, where one can cross on foot or on horseback.",
  "trincheira": "A ditch dug in the ground, with the earth piled up in front, which protects soldiers from enemy fire.",
  "paralela": "In the classic siege, a long trench dug parallel to the enemy's defenses. The besieger digs the first at a safe distance and then another closer, and sets up guns in each.",
  "abatis": "A barrier of cut trunks and branches, with the sharpened ends pointing outward, placed in front of a fortification to slow down attackers.",
  "cipaios": "Indian soldiers in the service of European armies, armed and trained in the European way, with British officers. Most of the army of the East India Company was made up of sepoys.",
  "maratas": "A people and confederation of warrior chiefs from west-central India, who dominated much of the subcontinent in the 18th century, after the decline of the Mughal Empire.",
  "pindaris": "Irregular horsemen from central India, who lived off plunder and served the Maratha chiefs.",
  "peshwa": "Title of the prime minister of the Marathas, which in practice became the hereditary ruler of the confederation. In 1803 it was Baji Rao II.",
  "companhia das índias orientais": "A British trading company which, with its own army, came to govern a large part of India. It was a business and a state at the same time.",
  "liga da neutralidade armada": "An alliance of countries of northern Europe (Russia, Denmark-Norway, Sweden and Prussia) to protect neutral trade against searches and blockades by the British navy.",
  "bloqueio naval": "Surrounding a country's ports with warships to prevent ships from entering or leaving.",
  "armistício": "An agreement to stop fighting for a time, while peace is negotiated.",
  "convenção nacional": "The elected assembly that governed France from September 1792 to 1795. It was the one that abolished the monarchy and proclaimed the Republic.",
  "primeira coalizão": "The alliance of European monarchies (Austria, Prussia and, later, Great Britain and others) against revolutionary France, from 1792.",
  "emigrados": "French nobles who fled the Revolution and joined foreign armies to try to restore the king.",
  "felás": "Peasants of Egypt. In the battle, many were forcibly recruited as infantry, without real military training.",
  "milícia": "Armed citizens who serve for short periods, without the career or the training of professional soldiers.",
  "hessianos": "German soldiers, generally from the principality of Hesse, rented out by their princes to the British government to fight in America.",
  "beys": "Mamluk chiefs of Egypt. They were the real rulers of the country, although the Ottoman sultan was the nominal lord."
});

Object.assign(DEEP, {
  yorktown: {
    war: "American War of Independence (1775–1783), Virginia campaign of 1781",
    hook: "A British army was trapped in a Virginia harbor because the fleet that was supposed to fetch it did not arrive in time.",
    chapters: [
      { n: "01", title: "The world before", blocks: [
        "In 1781, the war between the United States and Great Britain had already lasted six years, and nobody was winning. The Americans had declared independence in 1776, and France joined them in 1778, partly out of rivalry with Great Britain. The British, tired of fighting in the north, had taken the war to the south, where they hoped to count on the help of colonists loyal to the king.",
        "It was in the south that General Charles Cornwallis ran out of steam. After a costly victory at Guilford Courthouse, in North Carolina, he marched to Virginia and reached Petersburg on 20 May 1781. The orders he received from Henry Clinton, the British commander in New York, were confusing. He ended up sent to Yorktown, a town on the shore of Chesapeake Bay, to build a deep-water port there where the British navy could anchor.",
        { h: "The road to Yorktown" },
        { timeline: [
          ["20 May 1781", "Cornwallis arrives at Petersburg, in Virginia."],
          ["6 Jul", "Washington and Rochambeau, commander of the French army, meet at White Plains and discuss the next blow."],
          ["14 Aug", "A letter arrives from Admiral de Grasse: he will bring his fleet to Virginia, but can only stay until 14 October."],
          ["19 Aug", "The allied armies begin to march south."],
          ["5 Sep", "The French fleet defeats the British at the mouth of Chesapeake Bay."],
          ["28 Sep", "The allied army leaves Williamsburg and begins to surround Yorktown."],
          ["19 Oct", "Cornwallis surrenders."]
        ]},
        "Washington wanted to attack New York. De Grasse's letter changed everything: it was a chance to catch a British army in a place where the enemy navy could not rescue it, but only if everything went right within a few weeks. So that Clinton would not guess the destination, Washington used false dispatches suggesting an attack on New York. The whole march, counting from Newport, where the French were, was about 1,090 km (680 miles) and took 14 weeks."
      ]},
      { n: "02", title: "The main figures", blocks: [
        { h: "George Washington, 49 years old" },
        "Commander in chief of the Continental Army. At Yorktown, he struck the first blow of the pickaxe on the trench and fired the first cannon of the siege. Passing through Mount Vernon in September, he was seeing his home for the first time in six years.",
        { h: "The Count of Rochambeau, 56 years old" },
        "A veteran of the French army, he was sent by King Louis XVI in 1780 with about 5,300 men. He had to coordinate a French army and an American one with Washington, and the good relationship between the two was part of what made the alliance work. According to the account, when the British officer wanted to hand the sword over to him, he pointed to Washington.",
        { h: "The Count de Grasse" },
        "French admiral. It was he who decided to take the fleet to the Chesapeake and close the sea. Without that decision, there would have been no Yorktown.",
        { h: "Lord Cornwallis, 42 years old" },
        "A British general with a good military reputation, bold, but with weak orders and dependent on a navy that did not come. His army was sick: malaria struck his troops, and he himself estimated that half the men could not fight at the time of the surrender.",
        { h: "Other names" },
        "The Marquis de Lafayette, 24 years old, had spent the summer following Cornwallis with an American force that grew to about 4,500 men, and commanded the Light Division in the siege. Alexander Hamilton led the American assault on one of the redoubts. The Count of Deux-Ponts led the French one. On the British side, Colonel Charles O'Hara, who handed over Cornwallis's sword, and Banastre Tarleton, the most famous cavalryman of the British army in the south, who was wounded."
      ]},
      { n: "03", title: "Step by step", blocks: [
        { h: "The forces" },
        "The allies totaled between 17,000 and 19,000 men, according to the most used counts. The exact numbers vary: one count gives 8,000 to 9,000 Americans, of whom about 5,900 were regular soldiers and 3,100 [[militia|milícia]], and some 7,800 French on land. The British had between 8,000 and 9,000 men: about 5,000 British and fewer than 3,000 Germans ([[Hessians|hessianos]] and others). That is why some German historians call the siege 'the German battle'.",
        { h: "The trap at sea" },
        "On 5 September, de Grasse left the bay with 24 [[ships of the line|navio de linha]] and met Admiral Graves's squadron, with 19. The fight lasted about two hours and was tactical and inconclusive: the French aimed at masts and sails, and the British took more damage, about 90 dead and 246 wounded, and lost a ship, sunk afterward. But the strategic result was enormous. Barras's squadron arrived around 10 September with siege artillery, and Graves, after a council on 13 September, decided not to attack and went back to New York. Cornwallis was left with no way out by sea.",
        { h: "The terrain" },
        "Yorktown lies on the south bank of the York River, and across it, at Gloucester Point, the British had another battery covering the narrow passage of the river. Cornwallis's line had seven [[redoubts|reduto]] linked by earth trenches. The ground in front was marshy, and the allies had to build bridges to bring up the guns. Two redoubts were the key: number 10, near the river, and number 9, about 400 meters inland, protected by [[abatis|abatis]] and ditches.",
        { h: "The siege" },
        "Between 28 September and 5 October, the allies occupied positions abandoned by the British and prepared the batteries. On the night of 6 October they began the [[first parallel|paralela]], a trench of about 1.8 km, and on 9 October the batteries were ready, and Washington fired the first cannon. On 11 and 12 October they dug the second parallel, some 365 meters closer. From then on, the fire on the British redoubts and trenches was continuous.",
        { h: "The night of 14 October" },
        "To take the two redoubts, the allies attacked at night. Hamilton led about 400 American light infantry against redoubt 10, defended by some 70 men. The attack lasted about 25 minutes, with 9 Americans killed and 25 wounded. At the same time, about 400 French under Deux-Ponts cut through the abatis and took redoubt 9, defended by some 120. With both redoubts in their hands, the British line lay open to allied fire.",
        { h: "The end" },
        "On 15 October, Lieutenant Colonel Abercromby went out with some 350 men to spike the allied guns. The French drove them back. On the night of the 16th, Cornwallis attempted a desperate sortie: to take the army across the river, by boat, to Gloucester Point. The first wave crossed, but a storm caught the boats on the return, and the escape failed. On 17 October, a drummer and an officer with a white handkerchief appeared, and the fire stopped. The negotiations took place on 18 October, at the Moore house, and the surrender, on 19 October, at two in the afternoon."
      ]},
      { n: "04", title: "Legacy", blocks: [
        { h: "Result" },
        "Cornwallis claimed illness and did not appear at the ceremony. General O'Hara tried to hand the sword first to Rochambeau, who pointed to Washington, and Washington, in turn, indicated General Benjamin Lincoln. It was Lincoln, who had surrendered to the British at Charleston in 1780, who accepted the sword. In revenge, Washington denied the British the honors of war that they had denied Lincoln at Charleston: the soldiers marched with their flags furled.",
        "The allies captured about 7,000 to 8,000 soldiers (the count varies by source), 214 pieces of artillery and 24 transport ships. Allied losses were 88 killed and 301 wounded. British and German losses vary widely between the sources: from 142 to 309 killed and from 326 to 595 wounded.",
        { h: "Why it matters" },
        "Graves's British fleet arrived on 24 October, five days too late, and went back to New York. In London, Prime Minister Lord North is said to have exclaimed: 'Oh God, it is all over'. On 4 March 1782, the House of Commons voted to end offensive operations in America, and North's government fell on 20 March. The war went on longer at sea, but peace came with the Treaty of Paris, on 3 September 1783, which recognized the independence of the United States.",
        "Yorktown also shows what the alliance did: without the French fleet, without the French soldiers and siege artillery, the Americans alone could not have done it.",
        { h: "Curiosities and controversies" },
        { note: "The music: tradition says the British band played 'The World Turned Upside Down' during the surrender march. But no record from the time says which tunes were played, and the story only appears almost a hundred years later." },
        { note: "The sword: many people believe that Cornwallis's sword is in the White House. The historian Jerome Green says that O'Hara handed the sword to Lincoln, who returned it on the spot." },
        { note: "The enslaved: thousands of enslaved people had fled to the British lines, seeking freedom. On 25 October, Washington ordered the fugitives to be rounded up and returned to their masters. The historian Gregory Urwin says this turned the Continental soldiers into slave catchers." },
        { note: "The loyalists: Washington refused an article of the agreement that would have protected the colonists who had joined the British. But Article VIII let the ship Bonetta leave 'without examination', which may have allowed some loyalists and deserters to escape." }
      ]}
    ]
  },

  valmy: {
    war: "French Revolutionary Wars: War of the First Coalition (1792–1797)",
    hook: "An afternoon of cannon fire, with a few hundred dead, changed the course of a revolution.",
    chapters: [
      { n: "01", title: "The world before", blocks: [
        "In 1792, the French Revolution was three years old, and the kings of Europe were frightened. King Louis XVI, still on the throne, was viewed with suspicion in Paris, and France declared war on Austria in April. Prussia, the most respected military kingdom on the continent, joined Austria. This was the [[First Coalition|primeira coalizão]].",
        "The French army was in terrible shape. A good part of the officers, nobles, had fled the country as [[émigrés|emigrados]], and many of the soldiers were newly arrived volunteers, with little training. The first campaigns were disasters. The best thing that remained was the artillery, inherited from the king's army, with professional officers and gunners.",
        { h: "The invasion" },
        { timeline: [
          ["Apr. 1792", "France declares war on Austria."],
          ["25 Jul", "The Duke of Brunswick, the Prussian commander, signs a manifesto threatening Paris."],
          ["10 Aug", "The people of Paris storm the Tuileries Palace, and the king is arrested."],
          ["23 Aug", "The Prussians take Longwy."],
          ["2 Sep", "They take Verdun, the last fortress before Paris."],
          ["19 Sep", "Kellermann joins Dumouriez at Sainte-Menehould."],
          ["20 Sep", "Battle of Valmy."]
        ]},
        "After Verdun, the road to Paris, some 200 km away, seemed open. It was General Charles Dumouriez who decided to occupy the passes of the Argonne forest, a region of woods and hills, to bar the Prussians. The Prussians went around the position, and Dumouriez found himself obliged to turn his army. On 19 September, General François Kellermann arrived with his army at Sainte-Menehould and joined him. The two French armies totaled about 54,000 men. The Prussians, in going around, had left the French behind them, and turned to fight."
      ]},
      { n: "02", title: "The main figures", blocks: [
        { h: "The Duke of Brunswick, 57 years old" },
        "Commander of the coalition. He was a respected general, trained in the school of Frederick the Great, and known for caution. He had the king of Prussia, Frederick William II, beside him in the field. The manifesto that bore his name, in July, threatened Paris with destruction if the king were mistreated, and provoked the opposite: it radicalized the revolution.",
        { h: "François Kellermann, 57 years old" },
        "A veteran of many wars, he commanded the army that faced the Prussians directly. At Valmy, he had his horse killed under him. Later, Napoleon made him Duke of Valmy, in 1808.",
        { h: "Charles Dumouriez, 53 years old" },
        "He commanded the French army that occupied the Argonne and was the one who set up the defense. In 1793, after a defeat, he went over to the enemy side.",
        { h: "Other names" },
        "General d'Aboville commanded the French artillery. The Duke of Chartres, 18 years old, the future King Louis Philippe, commanded a brigade of dragoons and calmed battalions that had fled. And the poet Johann Wolfgang von Goethe, 43 years old, was with the Prussian army and watched the battle from the enemy field."
      ]},
      { n: "03", title: "Step by step", blocks: [
        { h: "The forces" },
        "Kellermann had about 36,000 men and 40 guns. The Prussians had about 34,000 and between 54 and 58 guns, depending on the source. The numbers are similar, but that is not the whole story: the Austrians and the émigrés of the coalition army were far away, late in their march, and only the Prussians faced the French. The French were a mixture of the king's soldiers, who knew how to fight, and volunteers who had never heard real gunfire.",
        { h: "The terrain" },
        "A region of low hills and chalk fields, in Champagne, muddy after days of rain. Kellermann placed his line, bowed outward in an arc, on the slopes near the village of Valmy, around a windmill. The Prussians occupied the hill of the Cabaret de la Lune, about 2.4 km from the windmill, from where they could see the French line about 1.2 km away.",
        { h: "In the morning" },
        "The morning began with rain and fog. The Prussian hussars, advancing blind, drew fire near the inn of La Lune, and that was the first shot of the day. General Chazot tried to retake the hill on Dumouriez's orders, but the Prussian guns arrived first. The hill became the anchor of the Prussian line. Kellermann ordered the Valmy windmill destroyed, so that the enemy would not use it as an aiming point.",
        { h: "The cannonade" },
        "In the late morning the wind carried the fog away, and the two lines saw each other. The Prussian artillery fired on the windmill. A shot killed Kellermann's horse, and several officers around him were killed or wounded. Around 2 p.m., a Prussian shell hit a French ammunition wagon, and the explosion struck two more. Two French infantry battalions fled, and the wagon drivers, recruited peasants, ran off with the loaded wagons. The Duke of Chartres reorganized the battalions, and d'Aboville brought more ammunition.",
        "Brunswick saw the confusion and ordered the infantry to advance, in columns. Under the fire of the French artillery, it advanced a little and was called back.",
        { h: "The shout" },
        "It was at this moment, according to tradition, that Kellermann raised his hat on the tip of his sword and shouted 'Long live the Nation!'. The soldiers repeated the cry, and sang the Marseillaise and the 'Ça ira'. The sources do not give the exact time. At 4 p.m., the Prussians formed up for a second advance against Valmy, which also stopped and was called off. Brunswick held a quick council with the king and the officers and said, according to the account: 'Here, we do not fight'. The fire stopped around 6 p.m., after about 20,000 cannon shots.",
        { note: "Losses were small: the most used counts give about 300 French and between 180 and 200 Prussians killed or wounded. For such a famous battle, it was a fight with very light casualties." }
      ]},
      { n: "04", title: "Legacy", blocks: [
        { h: "Result" },
        "That night, the French left their campfires lit and fell back to a line to the south. The Prussians, for their part, ran out of food and were struck by dysentery: about 12,000 of the 42,000 men fell ill, and many died. Brunswick waited ten days at La Lune and broke camp on 30 September. At the beginning of October, the Prussians gave Verdun back, and the coalition army left France.",
        "On the very day of the battle, 20 September, the [[National Convention|convenção nacional]] met in Paris. The next day, it abolished the monarchy, and the Republic was proclaimed soon afterward. In October, the French took Mainz, and in November, Dumouriez won the battle of Jemappes, in Belgium.",
        { h: "Why it matters" },
        "Valmy was not a big battle in terms of dead. It was big for what it meant: the first victory of the revolutionary army against the best troops in Europe, and proof that the Revolution was not going to fall at once. It became a symbol of the 'citizen army', although historians remind us that it was the professional soldiers, above all the gunners, who held the line.",
        { h: "Curiosities and controversies" },
        { note: "Goethe, who was in the Prussian camp, wrote in 1822, thirty years later, that he said that evening that from this place and this day a new epoch in the history of the world began, and that those who were there could say they had been present at its birth. The exact words vary from one source to another, and some historians doubt that the phrase was said at the time." },
        { note: "Why did Brunswick stop? Historians have not reached a single answer. Among the explanations: the strong French position, the reinforcements arriving, Prussia's concern about the Russian invasion of Poland and the duke's own caution. There is also the story that he was bribed or received a secret appeal from Louis XVI, but the historian Munro Price found no convincing proof." },
        { note: "The Valmy windmill: the most common version says Kellermann ordered it destroyed or burned on the day of the battle. Some accounts place it as the French command post, and the windmill seen today is a reconstruction." }
      ]}
    ]
  },

  piramides: {
    war: "Egyptian campaign (1798–1801)",
    hook: "An elite of horsemen with centuries of tradition against squares of rifles and cannon: it was over in an afternoon.",
    chapters: [
      { n: "01", title: "The world before", blocks: [
        "In 1798, revolutionary France was at war with Great Britain, and the government, the Directory, wanted to hit the enemy where it hurt: the road to India. The young general Napoleon Bonaparte, 28 years old, famous for his victories in Italy, proposed conquering Egypt. He left Toulon in May, took Malta on the way and landed near Alexandria in early July.",
        "Egypt was a province of the Ottoman Empire, but the ones who really ruled were the [[Mamluks|mamelucos]], a military caste that had governed the country for centuries. Their two chiefs, the [[beys]] Murad and Ibrahim, shared power in Cairo. The Mamluks were horsemen trained from childhood, in heavy armor, and had a fearsome reputation.",
        { h: "The road to the battle" },
        { timeline: [
          ["Jul. 1798", "The French land near Alexandria and take the city."],
          ["Jul. 1798", "The army crosses the desert toward Cairo, on a forced march, in the heat."],
          ["21 Jul", "Battle near Embabeh, on the west bank of the Nile."],
          ["24 Jul", "Napoleon enters Cairo."]
        ]},
        "Murad Bey decided to wait for the French on the west bank of the Nile, opposite Cairo. Ibrahim Bey stayed with his army on the other side of the river, on the east bank."
      ]},
      { n: "02", title: "The main figures", blocks: [
        { h: "Napoleon Bonaparte, 28 years old" },
        "A Corsican general, he commanded the army that had won in Italy. For him, Egypt was also an intellectual adventure: he took more than a hundred scientists, engineers and artists on the expedition. Before the battle, in the order of the day, he wrote the most famous phrase of the campaign: 'From the height of these pyramids, forty centuries look down upon you'.",
        { h: "Murad Bey" },
        "One of the two Mamluk chiefs. He commanded the Egyptian army in the field. His plan was simple: pin the French at the fortified ends and deliver the blow with the cavalry in the center.",
        { h: "The other names" },
        "On the French side, five divisions, from right to left, under Louis Desaix, Jean Reynier, Charles Dugua, Honoré Vial and Louis André Bon. On the Mamluk side, Ayyub Bey, the defterdar (treasurer), led the main attack."
      ]},
      { n: "03", title: "Step by step", blocks: [
        { h: "The forces" },
        "The French had between 20,000 and 25,000 men, according to historians: about 17,000 infantry, 3,000 cavalry and additional artillery. The Egyptian numbers are far more uncertain. One historian gives about 6,000 mounted Mamluks and 15,000 infantry; another, 6,000 Mamluks and 54,000 Arab irregulars; another, 50,000 men in total. The infantry were, for the most part, [[fellahin|felás]] forcibly recruited, of far lower quality than the French.",
        { h: "The terrain" },
        "The Nile plain, on the west bank, opposite Cairo. Murad anchored his right on the river, at the village of Embabeh, fortified with infantry and artillery. His left rested on the village of Biktil, also with guns. In the center, the Mamluk cavalry. The pyramids of Giza lay several kilometers away. Napoleon gave the battle their name, but in Egypt it is also called the battle of Embabeh.",
        { h: "The square" },
        { formation: true },
        "The French had marched all night. Each division formed a hollow [[square|quadrado]]: the infantry on all four sides, with the cavalry and baggage in the middle and the guns at the corners. The five squares advanced south, in echelon: the right (Desaix) slightly ahead, the left (Bon) protected by the Nile. It was a formation made against cavalry: cavalry cannot break a wall of bayonets, and musket and cannon fire from every side punishes whoever approaches.",
        { h: "The charges" },
        "Desaix sent a detachment to occupy Biktil, and some Mamluks tried to attack it, without success. Murad then launched the cavalry against the squares, charge after charge. An armored horseman came within a few steps of the French lines and challenged someone to a duel, and was shot down. Around 3:30 p.m., Murad ordered Ayyub Bey to lead a general attack. Desaix, Reynier and Dugua held.",
        { h: "Embabeh" },
        "While the Mamluk cavalry broke up against the squares, Bon's division organized into attack columns and stormed Embabeh. The garrison broke. Many tried to escape across the Nile, and hundreds drowned. Ibrahim, on the other bank, saw everything and could do nothing. Murad, wounded by a saber on one cheek, fled to Upper Egypt with a few thousand horsemen."
      ]},
      { n: "04", title: "Legacy", blocks: [
        { h: "Result" },
        "According to French reports, losses were 29 killed and 260 wounded. On the Egyptian side, estimates speak of thousands killed and wounded, and some historians reach 10,000, of whom about 7,000 were Mamluks killed. The numbers on both sides come from accounts by interested parties, and are probably imprecise.",
        "Napoleon entered Cairo on 24 July. The Mamluk army dispersed, and part of it fled to Syria. Murad still fought in Upper Egypt, until he was defeated by Desaix at the end of 1799.",
        { h: "Why it matters" },
        "It was the beginning of the end of Mamluk rule in Egypt. But the victory was short-lived: on 1 August, only eleven days later, Nelson destroyed the French fleet in Abukir Bay (the battle of the Nile), and Napoleon's army was cut off from France. The expedition also had a lasting scientific side: the scholars who accompanied it founded the Institute of Egypt, in Cairo, and in July 1799 French soldiers found, near Rosetta, the stone that would allow Champollion to decipher the hieroglyphs, more than twenty years later.",
        "Napoleon left Egypt in August 1799, without the army, and in November he was in power in France.",
        { h: "Curiosities and controversies" },
        { note: "The name: 'battle of the Pyramids' is a creation of Napoleon. The fight was at Embabeh, several kilometers from the pyramids, which could barely be seen." },
        { note: "The phrase: 'forty centuries look down upon you' is Napoleon's most famous line, but it is the version recorded in the order of the day and in memoirs. Nobody knows for sure how it was said aloud." },
        { note: "The film: Ridley Scott's 'Napoleon', from 2023, was criticized for the liberties it took in showing this battle." }
      ]}
    ]
  },

  copenhague: {
    war: "French Revolutionary Wars, the League of Armed Neutrality (1800–1801)",
    hook: "An admiral put the telescope to his blind eye and said he could not see the signal to retreat.",
    chapters: [
      { n: "01", title: "The world before", blocks: [
        "In 1801, Great Britain was at war with France and used its ships to blockade enemy ports and to search the ships of neutral countries. This irritated the countries of northern Europe, which lived off trade. In December 1800, Tsar Paul I of Russia headed an alliance: the [[League of Armed Neutrality|liga da neutralidade armada]], with Denmark-Norway, Sweden and Prussia.",
        "The British fear was that Denmark-Norway would ally itself fully with France, and that the Baltic fleets, together, would form a dangerous force. London decided to send a fleet to the Baltic to force Denmark out of the league.",
        { h: "The road to the battle" },
        { timeline: [
          ["12 Mar 1801", "The British fleet leaves Yarmouth, under Admiral Hyde Parker, with Nelson as second in command."],
          ["23–24 Mar", "Tsar Paul I is assassinated. The Danes do not yet know."],
          ["30 Mar", "The fleet passes the Sound. Kronborg castle fires, and the Swedish guns, on the other side, stay silent."],
          ["1 Apr", "Nelson's squadron goes down the outer channel and anchors about 3 km from the Danish line."],
          ["2 Apr", "Battle of Copenhagen."]
        ]},
        "Hyde Parker was cautious. Nelson wanted to attack soon, before the Danes could prepare. On the night of 30 March, at a council of war, most of the officers were for delay, but Nelson offered to attack with ten ships of the line, and Parker gave him twelve and the direction of the attack."
      ]},
      { n: "02", title: "The main figures", blocks: [
        { h: "Horatio Nelson, 42 years old" },
        "He was already the hero of the battle of the Nile, in 1798. Aggressive, popular with the sailors and given to disobedience when he thought he knew better. He lost the sight of his right eye at Calvi, in 1794, and that is why the episode of the signal became famous. His method was to give freedom of action to his captains, whom he knew and trusted.",
        { h: "Sir Hyde Parker" },
        "The commander of the fleet. He stayed with the reserve, at a distance, and from there watched the fight. Worried about the losses, he sent the signal to retreat. He was recalled to London on 5 May.",
        { h: "Olfert Fischer and Prince Frederick" },
        "Fischer commanded the Danish naval defense. On land, the one in charge was the crown prince Frederick, the regent. It was with him that Nelson negotiated the truce.",
        { h: "Other names" },
        "Captain Thomas Foley commanded the Elephant, Nelson's ship, and Thomas Hardy sounded the channel the night before. Captain Edward Riou, of the frigates, was killed in the battle."
      ]},
      { n: "03", title: "Step by step", blocks: [
        { h: "The forces" },
        "The British had 12 [[ships of the line|navio de linha]] in the attack, plus [[frigates|fragata]], smaller ships, bomb vessels and fireships. The Danes and Norwegians had seven ships of the line, four frigates and several smaller hulls, in addition to [[floating batteries|bateria flutuante]] and the Trekroner fort, at the harbor entrance. Many of the Danish ships were old hulks, without masts, used only as gun platforms, and that is part of what made the defense so hard to beat.",
        { h: "The terrain" },
        "The defense of Copenhagen was anchored along the King's Channel, a narrow, shallow passage with sandbanks, on the east side of the city. It was a place where large ships had little room and could run aground.",
        { h: "The night before" },
        "Hardy sounded the channel during the night. Nelson dictated the plan until about one in the morning: each British ship was to anchor opposite a Danish ship and fight it. The captains received their orders at 8 in the morning.",
        { h: "The attack" },
        { formation: true },
        "The ships weighed anchor at 9:30. The Edgar, in front, anchored opposite the Jylland, and the Ardent and the Glatton came behind, the Glatton opposite the Dannebrog, the Danish flagship. But the channel was treacherous: the Bellona and the Russell ran aground on the middle shoal, and the Agamemnon anchored too far away, to the east, and did not even join the fight. The Bellona and the Russell, even so, fired from the shoal. The Defiance reached her position opposite Trekroner around 11:30.",
        "The Danish batteries opened fire around 10:05, and by 11:30 the fighting was general. It was a fight of cannon against cannon, at short range, with the ships stationary.",
        { h: "The signal" },
        "Around 1:30 p.m., Parker, who watched the fight from afar and thought the losses were too high, hoisted the signal to retreat. An officer was sent to Nelson with authorization to withdraw if he thought best, but the signal went up before he arrived. According to Colonel Stewart's account, Nelson put the telescope to his blind eye and said he had a right to be blind sometimes, and ordered the signal for 'close action' to stay hoisted. It was a much-told story, and the exact words vary from one source to another.",
        "The captains of Riou's frigates, who could see Parker's signal better, obeyed and began to pull away. Riou's ship was hit, and he died.",
        { h: "The end" },
        "Around 2 p.m., the Danish fire grew weaker (some Danish historians say 2:30). Two Danish ships, the Nyborg and the Aggershuus, tried to flee to the harbor and sank. Nelson sent a letter to Prince Frederick, under a flag of truce, and around 3 p.m. the prince replied and the fire stopped. In a second letter, Nelson offered to return the Danish wounded and remove the unwounded prisoners, and to take or burn the ships that had surrendered. Around 4:30 p.m., the Dannebrog exploded, and more than 250 men died."
      ]},
      { n: "04", title: "Legacy", blocks: [
        { h: "Result" },
        "Of the 18 Danish ships involved, two sank, one blew up and 12 were captured. The British burned 11 of the captured ones and took one, the Holsteen, to England. Human losses vary between the sources. By the official British numbers, 274 killed and 737 wounded on their side; another source gives 253 and 688. On the Danish side, a 1994 study by the Royal Danish Naval Museum estimates 367 killed, 635 wounded and 1,779 prisoners; another account speaks of about 790 killed and 910 wounded.",
        { h: "Why it matters" },
        "Nelson went to Copenhagen to talk with the prince on 3 April. On 8 or 9 April, depending on the source, an [[armistice|armistício]] of 14 weeks (originally 16) was concluded, in which Denmark left the league. The assassination of the tsar had ended the league anyway, and the new tsar, Alexander, drew away from it. Parker was recalled, and Nelson took command. In May, he went as far as Reval, in Estonia, and found the Russian fleet withdrawn into Kronstadt. The definitive peace with Denmark was signed on 23 October 1801. Nelson was made a viscount.",
        "It was a costly and disputed victory, and it showed how far Nelson was willing to go.",
        { h: "Curiosities and controversies" },
        { note: "The eye: Nelson lost the sight of his right eye at Calvi, in 1794, but the eye still looked normal. Some modern analyses suggest the injury was less serious than the legend says." },
        { note: "Who won? The British kept the position and the truce, but the Danes fought bravely and the defense nearly beat them. Nelson himself is said to have called it the hardest battle of his life, but this phrase, in its exact form, does not appear in the sources we consulted." },
        { note: "Parker's order: some say the signal gave Nelson the option to continue. The sources we consulted say it was a general order to all ships, and that what Nelson did was disobey." }
      ]}
    ]
  },

  assaye: {
    war: "Second Anglo-Maratha War (1803–1805)",
    hook: "A few thousand regular soldiers attacked an army of tens of thousands with a hundred cannon, and Wellington never forgot it.",
    chapters: [
      { n: "01", title: "The world before", blocks: [
        "At the beginning of the 19th century, the [[British East India Company|companhia das índias orientais]] controlled Bengal, Madras and important parts of India, and the [[Marathas|maratas]] were the greatest rivals in its way. The Maratha confederation, governed by powerful chiefs such as Daulatrao Scindia, of Gwalior, and Raghuji II Bhonsle, of Berar, had armies trained by European officers, with European-style infantry and plenty of artillery.",
        "The quarrel broke out in December 1802, with the Treaty of Bassein. The [[peshwa|peshwa]] Baji Rao II, driven out of Poona by his rivals, signed with the British, who put him back on the throne under their protection. Scindia and Bhonsle saw this as a humiliation and joined forces near the border of the Nizam of Hyderabad, a British ally.",
        { h: "The road to Assaye" },
        { timeline: [
          ["Dec. 1802", "Treaty of Bassein: the British restore the peshwa in Poona."],
          ["Aug. 1803", "Scindia refuses to back down. Wellesley declares war on him and on Berar."],
          ["Sep. 1803", "Wellesley and Colonel Stevenson advance in separate columns, with the plan of joining to attack the Maratha army."],
          ["23 Sep", "Wellesley discovers that the Marathas are closer than expected and attacks without waiting for Stevenson."]
        ]},
        "The plan was to attack together on 24 September. But when Wellesley learned that the Maratha army was nearby, he chose not to wait. Later, the British official Thomas Munro would criticize him for it. Wellesley replied that the information he had received was wrong."
      ]},
      { n: "02", title: "The main figures", blocks: [
        { h: "Arthur Wellesley, 34 years old" },
        "Brother of the governor-general of India, he had an officer's career until then without big pitched battles. At Assaye, he had two horses killed under him. Later he would be the Duke of Wellington, the victor of Waterloo.",
        { h: "Daulatrao Scindia and Raghuji II Bhonsle" },
        "The Maratha chiefs of Gwalior and Berar. Scindia had a modern army, with infantry brigades trained by Europeans.",
        { h: "Anthony Pohlmann" },
        "The European officer who commanded the Maratha infantry trained in the European way. It was he who swung the line through 90 degrees when Wellesley crossed the river.",
        { h: "The other names" },
        "Colonel Patrick Maxwell commanded the British cavalry, the 19th Light Dragoons, and died in a charge. Lieutenant Colonel William Orrock, on the British side, misunderstood an order and disorganized the right of the line."
      ]},
      { n: "03", title: "Step by step", blocks: [
        { h: "The forces" },
        "Wellesley had about 4,500 regular infantry and cavalry soldiers, 17 guns and some 5,000 allied horsemen (from Mysore and elsewhere), who barely fought. They were the 74th and 78th regiments of highlanders, the 19th Light Dragoons and battalions of Madras [[sepoys|cipaios]]. The Marathas had about 50,000 men, with estimates up to 70,000: some 10,800 European-trained infantry, in three brigades, plus 10,000 to 20,000 irregular infantry from Berar, 30,000 to 40,000 irregular horsemen (many [[Pindaris|pindaris]]) and more than 100 guns.",
        { h: "The terrain" },
        "The Kaitna and Juah rivers framed the Maratha position. The only ford that Wellesley's guides knew lay right in front of the enemy line. But Wellesley noticed that two villages, Peepulgaon and Waroor, stood one on each side of the river, well beyond the Maratha left, and deduced that there must be a ford there. His chief engineer, Captain John Johnson, went to check and confirmed it.",
        { h: "The crossing" },
        { formation: true },
        "Around 3 p.m., the British crossed unopposed, at that ford, and formed a line on the north bank, on the enemy's left side. Pohlmann reacted quickly: he swung the Maratha line through 90 degrees to face the new enemy, and Wellesley extended his own to keep pace.",
        { h: "The artillery" },
        "The Maratha guns did enormous damage to the advancing soldiers and put the British guns out of action. Wellesley ordered the artillery abandoned and had the infantry advance with fixed [[bayonets|baioneta]], straight at the enemy guns.",
        { h: "The left and the right" },
        "On the British left, the highlanders of the 78th and the Madras battalions reached the Maratha guns and broke the enemy right, and the rest of the line gave way. But on the right it went wrong: Lieutenant Colonel Orrock misread the orders and led his men toward the village of Assaye, opening a hole in the line. The pickets were nearly destroyed, and the 74th formed a square to defend itself.",
        "Colonel Maxwell, with the 19th Light Dragoons, attacked and saved the 74th, pushing the Maratha troops back across the Juah River.",
        { h: "The dead who rose" },
        "Some Maratha gunners had lain down pretending to be dead. When the British passed, they went back to the guns and fired into the rear of the 74th. Wellesley himself led a cavalry charge to retake the pieces. Maxwell died in a later charge, hit by grapeshot. Around 6 p.m., the irregular infantry of Berar left the village of Assaye. The Maratha cavalry, mostly Pindaris, barely entered the fight. The British were too exhausted to pursue. The allied cavalry refused to go alone."
      ]},
      { n: "04", title: "Legacy", blocks: [
        { h: "Result" },
        "The British lost 428 killed, 1,138 wounded and 18 missing, more than a third of the men engaged. The Marathas lost 98 guns and seven flags. As for their casualties, the British report speaks of about 1,200 killed, and some modern historians calculate about 6,000 killed and wounded. Stevenson only arrived on the night of the next day, 24 September.",
        "Wellesley continued the campaign with victories at Argaon and Gawilghur, and the Maratha chiefs asked for peace.",
        { h: "Why it matters" },
        "It was Wellesley's first great victory and helped extend British dominion over India. The 74th earned the nickname 'the Assaye regiment', and the battle honor was granted to the units that fought in it.",
        "Wellesley called Assaye the bloodiest battle, for the number of men, that he had ever seen. Late in life, according to recorded conversations, he considered Assaye the best thing he did in combat.",
        { h: "Curiosities and controversies" },
        { note: "The numbers: the estimates of the forces vary. The 4,500 regulars are the most used count; adding the allied horsemen, one reaches about 9,500. The estimates of Maratha casualties, for their part, vary between 1,200 and 6,000." },
        { note: "The ford: the story that Wellesley deduced the existence of the ford on seeing the two villages is the most told. One source says he himself found the passage, another that the engineer Johnson confirmed it after the deduction." },
        { note: "The decision to attack: Thomas Munro criticized Wellesley for not waiting for Stevenson and for the heavy losses. Wellesley defended himself by saying that the information about the enemy position was wrong." }
      ]}
    ]
  },

  trafalgar: {
    war: "Napoleonic Wars: War of the Third Coalition (1805)",
    hook: "Nelson gave up the classic line of battle, and Great Britain became mistress of the sea for a century.",
    chapters: [
      { n: "01", title: "The world before", blocks: [
        "In 1805, Napoleon was at war with Great Britain and had a plan: to invade England. He gathered a large army near Boulogne, on the coast of the English Channel. One thing was missing: crossing the Channel required the French fleet to command the sea for a few days, and the Royal Navy was the largest navy in the world.",
        "The French fleet tried to deceive the British one: it went to the Caribbean and back, and joined the Spanish fleet. On 22 July, it fought an inconclusive action off Cape Finisterre, and Vice Admiral Pierre-Charles Villeneuve took refuge in Cádiz. The British sent Nelson to take charge of the blockade, and he took command on 28 September.",
        { h: "The road to Trafalgar" },
        { timeline: [
          ["22 Jul 1805", "Action off Cape Finisterre. Villeneuve takes refuge in Cádiz."],
          ["28 Sep", "Nelson arrives and takes over the blockade of Cádiz."],
          ["9 Oct", "Nelson writes the memorandum with his plan of attack to the captains."],
          ["18–20 Oct", "Villeneuve leaves Cádiz with the Franco-Spanish fleet."],
          ["21 Oct", "Battle of Trafalgar."]
        ]},
        "Villeneuve was reluctant to sail. His crews were poorly trained and supplies were short. But Napoleon wanted him to head for the Mediterranean, and, according to most accounts, he also sailed on learning that he was to be replaced. In the meantime, the army at Boulogne had already been sent against Austria, and the invasion of England was, in practice, abandoned."
      ]},
      { n: "02", title: "The main figures", blocks: [
        { h: "Horatio Nelson, 47 years old" },
        "A vice admiral, the most famous officer of the British navy. He had lost the sight of one eye and an arm in combat. He was bold and charismatic, and his command style was to give the general idea and let the captains decide: 'No captain can do very wrong if he places his ship alongside that of an enemy', he told them. After Trafalgar, he became Great Britain's greatest naval hero.",
        { h: "Pierre-Charles Villeneuve, 42 years old" },
        "A French vice admiral. He had survived the battle of the Nile, in 1798, and knew the strength of the British. He had little desire to face the Royal Navy. He was captured at Trafalgar. Released in 1806, he was found dead in a hotel room, in circumstances that the official story called suicide.",
        { h: "The other names" },
        "Cuthbert Collingwood, 57 years old, commanded the second British column in the Royal Sovereign. Captain Thomas Hardy commanded the Victory. The Spanish admiral Federico Gravina was second in the allied fleet and came out wounded. Rear Admiral Pierre Dumanoir commanded the Franco-Spanish van. Captain Jean-Jacques Lucas commanded the Redoutable, famous for its fight alongside the Victory."
      ]},
      { n: "03", title: "Step by step", blocks: [
        { h: "The forces" },
        "Great Britain had 27 [[ships of the line|navio de linha]], with about 2,148 guns and 17,000 men. The Franco-Spanish fleet had 33, with somewhere between 2,568 and 2,632 guns and about 30,000 men, including some of the largest ships in the world, such as the Spanish Santísima Trinidad, with four decks.",
        { h: "The plan" },
        "The classic [[line of battle|linha de batalha]] put the two fleets side by side, in parallel lines, trading fire broadside to broadside. It was a way of fighting that almost never produced a decisive victory. Nelson decided to do it differently. He divided the fleet into two columns and attacked head-on, perpendicular to the enemy line, to cut it into three pieces. The order of sailing was already the order of battle, so as not to lose time forming a line.",
        "The cost was high: the leading ships would take fire without being able to reply, because their guns are on the sides. Nelson ordered the use of all available canvas to shorten that time. One source calls the plan a 'well-calculated gamble'.",
        { h: "The morning" },
        { formation: true },
        "Around 5:40 in the morning, the British were about 21 miles northwest of Cape Trafalgar. At 8 o'clock, Villeneuve ordered the fleet to wear round and return to Cádiz, which left the line stretched and uneven. The wind was very light and the sea had a strong swell, which rocked the ships and spoiled the aim of the French and Spanish. The ships crawled. Nelson's column, the windward one, had the Victory in front, followed by Téméraire, Conqueror and Neptune. Collingwood's, the [[leeward|sotavento]] one, had the Royal Sovereign in front and the Belleisle second.",
        "Around 11:45, Nelson sent the signal 'England expects that every man will do his duty'. It was a modified version: Nelson had written 'confides', and the officer Pasco suggested 'expects'. It took 12 hoists of flags.",
        { h: "The Royal Sovereign" },
        "At noon, the French Fougueux fired the first shot against the Royal Sovereign. Collingwood is said to have told his officers: 'Let us do something today which the world may talk of hereafter'. The Royal Sovereign left the rest of the fleet behind and cut the allied line just astern of the Santa Ana, flagship of the Spanish admiral Álava.",
        { h: "The Victory" },
        "The Victory spent about 40 minutes under the fire of several enemy ships without being able to reply. Her wheel was smashed by a shot, and the ship had to be steered from below, at the tiller. At 12:45, she passed between the Bucentaure and the Redoutable and fired, at close range and with triple shot, a [[raking broadside|fogo em enfiada]] into the stern of the Bucentaure, Villeneuve's flagship. The enemy fleet was cut.",
        "The Victory ended up locked to the Redoutable, whose men tried to board the British ship. French grenades drove the gunners from the deck, and the Victory stopped firing for a time. The Téméraire came up on the Redoutable's bow and fired a [[carronade|carronada]] at the French on the deck, just as they were preparing to [[board|abordagem]]. It caused many casualties.",
        { h: "The death of Nelson" },
        "A marksman in the mast of the Redoutable hit Nelson in the left shoulder. The ball went through the spine and stopped near the right shoulder blade. Nelson was taken below. The sources do not give the exact time of the shot: the most common account is between 1:15 and 1:30 p.m. He died at 4:30, knowing that the victory was won. He told the surgeon Beatty: 'Thank God I have done my duty'. The chaplain Scott noted as his last words: 'God and my country'.",
        { h: "The end of the battle" },
        "The Redoutable surrendered at 1:55 p.m., with 99 fit men out of a crew of 643. The Santísima Trinidad surrendered after three hours of fighting. Dumanoir, who commanded the van, barely fought and left with four ships (Formidable, Scipion, Duguay-Trouin and Mont-Blanc), which would be captured on 4 November off Cape Ortegal. Gravina, wounded, returned to Cádiz with the ships he could."
      ]},
      { n: "04", title: "Legacy", blocks: [
        { h: "Result" },
        "On the day of the battle, the most used count is 17 allied ships captured and 1 destroyed, the Achille, which blew up. Adding Dumanoir's four, that is 22 of the 33 ships lost in all. The British lost no ships. Human losses were 458 killed and 1,208 wounded for the British and 4,395 killed, 2,541 wounded and 7,000 to 8,000 prisoners for the Franco-Spanish.",
        "Afterward, a storm destroyed most of the captured ships. Some sank, others were burned or deliberately sunk, and some were retaken in the confusion. On the allied side, 11 ships reached Cádiz, but only a few were in condition to sail. Gravina died months later, as a result of his wounds.",
        { h: "Why it matters" },
        "Trafalgar ended any real chance of Napoleon invading England, and confirmed British command of the seas, which was not seriously challenged for more than a century. Napoleon answered on land: on 2 December 1805, he won at Austerlitz and, later, tried to smother Great Britain with the Continental blockade.",
        "Lieutenant Lapenotière carried the news of the victory (and of Nelson's death) on a small ship, the Pickle, to Falmouth, and from there overland, by carriage, to the Admiralty in London.",
        { h: "Curiosities and controversies" },
        { note: "The signal: the most famous phrase in English naval history was 'England expects that every man will do his duty'. The original, written by Nelson, said 'confides'. The change was a suggestion of the signal officer. The phrase served as a model for Admiral Barroso's signal at Riachuelo, in 1865, in Brazil." },
        { note: "Was it tactics or was it preparation? Nelson's plan was risky, and the slow approach cost the first ships dearly. But there are historians who attribute the difference in losses more to the training, readiness and morale of the British crews than to Nelson's tactics. French and Spanish gunnery, affected by the heavy sea and little training, was indeed poor." },
        { note: "The body: according to tradition, Nelson's body was preserved in a barrel of brandy for the voyage back to England." }
      ]}
    ]
  }
});

